# POL degraded-mode removal-reference predicate — SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH.
#
# This file holds the word list the removal policy (hooks/lib/bash_safety_rules.py
# removal_policy) asks of a command it cannot prove safe. It is asked of
# DIFFERENT TEXT on the two paths, which are two rules (owner's rule,
# 2026-11-19):
#   primary  - the execution-boundary analyzer answered UNRESOLVED, having
#              already refused every removal it sees where it would run. The
#              list is asked only of the text that can become a command the
#              analyzer left unresolved (its `unresolved_scopes`): never of a
#              heredoc written to a file, another command's arguments, a
#              variable name, a comment or a message.
#   degraded - the analyzer gave no usable verdict, or the parser could not
#              read the command: the list is asked of the whole raw text.
# A match refuses; no match lets the command run and hands it to the record.
# The differential test (AC-R02-10) reads the SAME file. A copy of the pattern
# in any consumer is forbidden.
#
# The bare library names (remove, rmtree, unlinkSync, rm_rf, ...) are here
# because of QA finding F5: python3 -c 'import os; getattr(os,"remove")("X")'
# carries no rm/unlink/shred/srm token at all, so a front-end-only pattern left
# the interpreter dynamic-access family with no backstop on either path.
#
# Format: comment lines start with '#'; the first non-comment, non-blank line is
# the POSIX ERE. If this file is missing or yields no pattern, the hook DENIES.
(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])(rm|unlink|shred|srm|rmdir|rmtree|rimraf|unlinkSync|rmSync|rmdirSync|removeSync|remove|removedirs|remove_tree|rm_rf|rm_r|delete|deleteIfExists)([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)
